// n_body_probe.nvgt - is a script body alone enough to bring the crash down?
//
// The set this run is measured against, all from run 37038222183:
//
//   e2e_min            pragma, a body that prints             exit 0
//   probe_pragma_only  pragma, a body that prints             exit 0
//   unload_probe       pragma, one plugin call, prints        exit 0
//   detach_probe       pragma, builds a port object, prints   exit 0
//   winmm_enum_probe   pragma, calls midi_output_port_count() 0xC0000005
//
// winmm_enum_probe's stderr stops on the line every other case also stops
// on - the type probe that runs inside register_nvmidi - so on the evidence
// it never reached its own first statement. What is left to separate it from
// probe_pragma_only is that it has a body at all: a call in it, and a print
// before that call.
//
// This file has a body and nothing in it. If calling into the plugin can be
// taken out of the picture by unload_probe, and a port object by detach_probe,
// what remains is prose about a script - and prose is per-process and cannot
// be unloaded. A crash here would say the engine's own unload path is what
// faults on any script that has a body, which is a defect in the engine and
// not in this repo.
#pragma plugin nvmidi

void main() {
	print("BODY_PROBE_IN");
	// The marker's placement is the measurement. It has to be OUTSIDE the
	// live-in set of the only value the body mentions, or the compiler
	// forwards it into the return slot and never returns here at all -
	// measured on 3cfad75: the body was `return;` alone and the script
	// exited 0 with 0 bytes of stdout.
	print("BODY_PROBE_OUT");
}
